\id JOB - Brenton English Septuagint 
\h Job 
\toc1 Job 
\toc2 Job 
\toc3 Job 
\mt1 JOB 
\c 1 
\p
\v 1 \sc There\sc* was a certain man in the land of Ausis, whose name \add was\add* Job; and that man was true, blameless, righteous, \add and\add* godly, abstaining from everything evil. 
\v 2 He had seven sons and three daughters. 
\v 3 His cattle consisted of seven thousand sheep, three thousand camels, five hundred yoke of oxen, five hundred she-donkeys in the pastures, and a very great household, and he had a great estates in the earth; and that man was \add most\add* noble of the \add men\add* of the east. 
\p
\v 4 His sons visiting one another prepared a banquet every day, taking with them also their three sisters to eat and drink with them. 
\v 5 When the days of the banquet were completed, Job sent and purified them, having risen up in the morning, and offered sacrifices for them, according to their number, and one calf for a sin-offering for their souls: for Job said, Lest perhaps my sons have thought evil in their minds against God. Thus then Job did continually. 
\p
\v 6 It happened on a day, that, behold, the angels of God came to stand before the Lord, and the devil came with them. 
\v 7 The Lord said to the devil, Where have you come from? And the devil answered the Lord, and said, I have come from going around the earth, and walking up and down in the world. 
\v 8 The Lord said to him, Have you diligently considered my servant Job, that there is no one like him on the earth, a man blameless, true, godly, abstaining from everything evil? 
\v 9 Then the devil answered, and said before the Lord, Does Job worship the Lord for nothing? 
\v 10 Have you not made a hedge about him, and about his household, and all his possessions all around? And have you not blessed the works of his hands, and multiplied his cattle upon the land? 
\v 11 But put forth your hand, and touch all that he has: truly he will \f + \fr 1:11 \ft The verb “bless” is used euphemistically here, with the implied meaning “curse” or “renounce”.\f*bless you to \add your\add* face. 
\v 12 Then the Lord said to the devil, Behold, I give into your hand all that he has, but don't touch him. So the devil went out from the presence of the Lord. 
\p
\v 13 It happened on a certain day, that Job's sons and his daughters were drinking wine in the house of their elder brother. 
\v 14 And, behold, there came a messenger to Job, and said to him, The yokes of oxen were ploughing, and the she-donkeys were feeding near them; 
\v 15 and raiders came and took them for a prey, and killed the servants with the sword; and I alone have escaped to come to tell you. 
\v 16 While he was still speaking, there came another messenger, and said to Job, Fire has fallen from heaven, and burnt up the sheep, and devoured the shepherds likewise; and I alone have escaped to come to tell you. 
\v 17 While he was still speaking, there came another messenger, and said to Job, The horsemen formed three companies against us, and surrounded the camels, and took them for a prey, and killed the servants with the sword; and I alone have escaped to come to tell you. 
\v 18 While he is still speaking, another messenger comes, saying to Job, While your sons and your daughters were eating and drinking with their elder brother, 
\v 19 suddenly a great wind came on from the desert, and caught the four corners of the house, and the house fell upon your children, and they are dead; and I alone have escaped to come to tell you. 
\p
\v 20 So Job arose, and tore his garments, and shaved the hair of his head, and fell on the earth, and worshipped, 
\v 21 and said, I myself came forth naked from my mother's womb, and naked I will return there: the Lord gave, the Lord has taken away: as it seemed good to the Lord, so has it come to pass; blessed be the name of the Lord. 
\v 22 In all these events that befell him Job didn't sin at all before the Lord, and did not impute folly to God. 
\c 2 
\p
\v 1 It happened on a certain day, that the angels of God came to stand before the Lord, and the devil came among them to stand before the Lord. 
\v 2 The Lord said to the devil, Where have you come from? Then the devil said before the Lord, I have come from going through the world, and walking about the whole earth. 
\v 3 The Lord said to the devil, Have you then observed my servant Job, that there is no one of \add men\add* upon the earth like him, a harmless, true, blameless, godly man, abstaining from all evil? And he yet cleaves to innocence, since you have told \add me\add* to destroy his substance without cause? 
\v 4 The devil answered and said to the Lord, Skin for skin, a man will give all that he has as a ransom for his life. 
\v 5 No, but put forth your hand, and touch his bones and his flesh: truly he will \f + \fr 2:5 \ft The verb “bless” is used euphemistically here, with the implied meaning “curse” or “renounce”.\f*bless you to \add your\add* face. 
\v 6 The Lord said to the devil, Behold, I deliver him up to you; only save his life. 
\p
\v 7 So the devil went out from the Lord, and struck Job with sore boils from \add his\add* feet to \add his\add* head. 
\v 8 He took a potsherd to scrape away the discharge, and sat upon a dung-heap outside the city. 
\p
\v 9 When much time had passed, his wife said to him, How long will you hold out, saying, 
\v 9a Behold, I wait yet a little while, expecting the hope of my deliverance? 
\v 9b for, behold, your memorial is abolished from the earth, \add even your\add* sons and daughters, the pangs and pains of my womb which I bore in vain with sorrows; 
\v 9c and you yourself sit down to spend the nights in the open air among the corruption of worms, 
\v 9d and I am a wanderer and a servant from place to place and house to house, waiting for the setting of the sun, that I may rest from my labours and my pangs which now beset me: 
\v 9e but say some word against the Lord, and die. 
\v 10 But he looked on her, and said to her, You have spoken like one of the foolish women. If we have received good things of the hand of the Lord, will we not endure evil things? 
\p In all these things that happened to him, Job didn't sin at all with his lips before God. 
\p
\v 11 Now his three friends having heard of all the evil that had come upon him, came to him each from his own country: Eliphaz the king of the Thæmans, Baldad sovereign of the Saucheans, Sophar king of the Minæans: and they came to him with one accord, to comfort and to visit him. 
\v 12 When they saw him from a distance they did not know him; and they cried with a loud voice, and wept, and every one tore his garment, and sprinkled dust upon \add their heads\add*, 
\v 13 and they sat down beside him seven days and seven nights, and no one of them spoke; for they saw that \add his\add* affliction was dreadful and very great. 
\c 3 
\p
\v 1 After this Job opened his mouth, and cursed his day, 
\v 2 saying, 
\p
\v 3 Let the day perish in which I was born, and that night in which they said, Behold a man-child! 
\v 4 Let that night be darkness, and don't let the Lord regard it from above, neither let light come upon it. 
\v 5 But let darkness and the shadow of death seize it; let blackness come upon it; 
\v 6 let that day and night be cursed, let darkness carry them away; don't let it come into the days of the year, neither let it be numbered with the days of the months. 
\v 7 But let that night be pain, and don't let mirth come upon it, nor joy. 
\v 8 But let him that curses that day curse it, \add even\add* he who is ready to attack the monstrous sea-creature. 
\v 9 Let the stars of that night be darkened; let it remain \add dark\add*, and not come into light; and don't let it see the morning star arise: 
\v 10 because it didn't shut up the gates of my mother's womb, for \add so\add* it would have removed sorrow from my eyes. 
\p
\v 11 For why did I not die in the belly? And \add why\add* did I not come forth from the womb and die immediately? 
\v 12 Why did the knees support me? And why did I suck the breasts? 
\v 13 Now I should have lain down and been quiet, I should have slept and been at rest, 
\v 14 with kings \add and\add* councillors of the earth, who gloried in \add their\add* swords; 
\v 15 or with rulers, whose gold was abundant, who filled their houses with silver: 
\v 16 or \add I should have been\add* as an untimely birth proceeding from his mother's womb, or as infants who never saw light. 
\v 17 There the ungodly have burnt out the fury of rage; there the wearied in body rest. 
\v 18 The men of old time have together ceased to hear the exactor's voice. 
\v 19 The small and great are there, and the servant that feared his lord. 
\p
\v 20 For why is light given to those who are in bitterness, and life to the souls which are in griefs? 
\v 21 who desire death, and didn't obtain it, digging \add for it\add* as \add for\add* treasures; 
\v 22 and would be very joyful if they should gain it? 
\v 23 Death \add is\add* rest to \add such\add* a man, for God has hedged him in. 
\v 24 For my groaning comes before my food, and I weep, being beset with terror. 
\v 25 For the terror of which I meditated has come upon me, and that which I had feared has befallen me. 
\v 26 I was not at peace, nor quiet, I had no rest; yet wrath came upon me. 
\c 4 
\p
\v 1 Then Eliphaz the Thæmanite answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 Have you been often spoken to in distress? But who will endure the force of your words? 
\v 3 For because you have instructed many, and have strengthened the hands of the weak one, 
\v 4 and have supported the failing with words, and have imparted courage to feeble knees. 
\v 5 Yet now \add that\add* pain has come upon you, and touched you, you are troubled. 
\v 6 Isn't your fear \add founded\add* in folly, your hope also, and the mischief of your way? 
\v 7 Remember then who has perished, being pure? Or when were the true-hearted completely destroyed? 
\v 8 Accordingly as I have seen men ploughing barren places, and those who sow them will reap sorrows for themselves. 
\v 9 They will perish by the command of the Lord, and will be completely consumed by the breath of his wrath. 
\p
\v 10 The strength of the lion, and the voice of the lioness, and the exulting cry of snakes are quenched. 
\v 11 The old lion has perished for want of food, and the lions' whelps have forsaken one another. 
\p
\v 12 But if there had been any truth in your words, none of these evils would have befallen you. Will not my ear receive excellent \add revelations\add* from him? 
\v 13 But \add as when\add* terror falls upon men, with dread and a sound in the night, 
\v 14 horror and trembling seized me, and caused all my bones greatly to shake. 
\v 15 A spirit came before my face; and my hair and flesh quivered. 
\v 16 I arose and perceived it not: I looked, and there was no form before my eyes: but I only heard a breath and a voice, \add saying\add*, 
\v 17 What, will a mortal be pure before the Lord? Or a man be blameless in regard to his works? 
\v 18 Because he doesn't trust in his servants, and perceives perverseness in his angels. 
\p
\v 19 But \add as for\add* those who dwell in houses of clay, of whom we also are formed of the same clay, he smites them like a moth. 
\v 20 From morning to evening they no longer exist: they have perished, because they cannot help themselves. 
\v 21 For he blows upon them, and they are withered: they have perished for lack of wisdom. 
\c 5 
\p
\v 1 But call, if any one will listen to you, or if you will see any of the holy angels. 
\v 2 For wrath destroys the foolish one, and envy slays him that has gone astray. 
\v 3 I have seen foolish ones taking root: but suddenly their habitation was devoured. 
\v 4 Let their children be far from safety, and let them be crushed at the doors of vile men, and let there be no deliverer. 
\v 5 For what they have collected, the just will eat; but they will not be delivered out of calamities: let their strength be completely exhausted. 
\v 6 For labour cannot by any means come out of the earth, nor will trouble spring out of the mountains: 
\v 7 yet man is born to labour, and \add even so\add* the vulture's young seek the high places. 
\p
\v 8 Nevertheless I will beseech the Lord, and will call upon the Lord, the sovereign of all; 
\v 9 who does great things and untraceable, glorious things also, and marvellous, of which there is no number: 
\v 10 who gives rain upon the earth, sending water on the earth: 
\v 11 who exalts the lowly, and raises up those who are lost: 
\v 12 frustrating the counsels of the crafty, and their hands will not perform the truth: 
\v 13 who takes the wise in their wisdom, and subverts the counsel of the crafty. 
\v 14 In the day darkness will come upon them, and let them grope in the noon-day even as in the night: 
\v 15 and let them perish in war, and let the weak escape from the hand of the mighty. 
\v 16 Let the weak have hope, but the mouth of the unjust be stopped. 
\p
\v 17 But blessed \add is\add* the man whom the Lord has reproved; and don't reject the chastening of the Almighty. 
\v 18 For he causes \add a man\add* to be in pain, and restores \add him\add* again: he smites, and his hands heal. 
\v 19 Six times he will deliver you out of distresses: and in the seventh harm will not touch you. 
\v 20 In famine he will deliver you from death: and in war he will free you from the power of the sword. 
\v 21 He will hide you from the scourge of the tongue: and you will not be afraid of coming evils. 
\v 22 You will laugh at the unrighteous and the lawless: and you will not be afraid of wild beasts. 
\v 23 For the wild beasts of the field will be at peace with you. 
\v 24 Then you will know that your house will be at peace, and the provision for your tent will not fail. 
\v 25 You will know that your seed \add will be\add* abundant; and your children will be like the herbage of the field. 
\v 26 You will come to the grave like ripe grain reaped in its season, or as a heap of the grain-flour collected in proper time. 
\p
\v 27 Behold, we have thus sought out these matters; these are what we have heard: but do you reflect with yourself, if you have done anything \add wrong\add*. 
\c 6 
\p
\v 1 But Job answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 Oh that one would indeed weigh the wrath that is upon me, and take up my griefs in a balance together! 
\v 3 Truly they would be heavier than the sand by the seashore: but, as it seems, my words are vain. 
\v 4 For the arrows of the Lord are in my body, whose violence drinks up my blood: whenever I am going to speak, they pierce me. 
\v 5 What then? Will the wild ass bray for nothing, if he isn't seeking food? Or again, will the ox low at the manger, when he has fodder? 
\v 6 Will bread be eaten without salt? Or again, is there taste in empty words? 
\v 7 For my wrath cannot cease; for I perceive my food as the smell of a lion \add to be\add* loathsome. 
\p
\v 8 For oh that he would grant \add my desire\add*, and my petition might come, and the Lord would grant my hope! 
\v 9 Let the Lord begin and wound me, but don't let him completely destroy me. 
\v 10 Let the grave be my city, upon the walls of which I have leaped: I will not shrink from it; for I have not denied the holy words of my God. 
\v 11 For what is my strength, that I continue? What is my time, that my soul endures? 
\v 12 Is my strength the strength of stones? Or is my flesh of brass? 
\v 13 Or have I not trusted in him? But help is \add far\add* from me. 
\p
\v 14 Mercy has rejected me; and the visitation of the Lord has disregarded me. 
\v 15 My nearest relations have not regarded me; they have passed me by like a failing torrent, or like a passing surge. 
\v 16 They who used to reverence me, now have come against me like snow or congealed ice. 
\v 17 When it has melted at the approach of heat, it isn't known what it was. 
\v 18 Thus I also have been deserted of all; and I am ruined, and have become an outcast. 
\v 19 Behold the ways of the Thæmanites, you who mark the paths of the Sabæans. 
\v 20 They too that trust in cities and riches will come to shame. 
\v 21 But you also have come to me without pity; so that beholding my wound you are afraid. 
\v 22 What? Have I made any demand of you? Or do I ask for strength from you, 
\v 23 to deliver me from enemies, or to rescue me from the hand of the mighty ones? 
\p
\v 24 Teach me, and I will be silent: if in anything I have erred, tell me. 
\v 25 But as it seems, the words of a true man are vain, because I do not ask strength of you. 
\v 26 Neither will your reproof cause me to cease my words, for neither will I endure the sound of your speech. 
\v 27 Even because you attack the fatherless, and insult your friend. 
\v 28 But now, having looked upon your countenances, I will not lie. 
\v 29 Sit down now, and don't let there be unrighteousness; and unite again with the just. 
\v 30 For there is no injustice in my tongue; and does not my throat meditate understanding? 
\c 7 
\p
\v 1 Isn't the life of man upon earth a state of trial? And his existence as that of a hireling by the day? 
\v 2 Or as a servant that fears his master, and one who has grasped a shadow? Or as a hireling waiting for his pay? 
\v 3 So have I also endured months of vanity, and nights of pain have been appointed me. 
\v 4 Whenever I lie down, I say, When \add will it be\add* day? And whenever I rise up, again \add I say\add* when \add will it be\add* evening? And I am full of pains from evening to morning. 
\v 5 My body is covered with loathsome worms; and I waste away, scraping off clods of dust from my eruption. 
\v 6 My life is lighter than a word, and has perished in vain hope. 
\v 7 Remember then that my life is breath, and my eye will not see good any more. 
\v 8 The eye of him that sees me will not see me \add again\add*: your eyes are upon me, and I am no more. 
\v 9 \add I am\add* as a cloud that is cleared away from the sky: for if a man go down to the grave, he will not come up again: 
\v 10 and he will surely not return to his own house, neither will his place know him any more. 
\v 11 Then neither will I refrain my mouth: I will speak being in distress; being in anguish I will disclose the bitterness of my soul. 
\p
\v 12 Am I a sea, or a sea monster, that you have set a watch over me? 
\v 13 I said that my bed should comfort me, and I would privately counsel with myself on my couch. 
\v 14 You scare me with dreams, and terrify me with visions. 
\v 15 You will separate life from my spirit; and yet \add keep\add* my bones from death. 
\v 16 For I will not live forever, that I should patiently endure: depart from me, for my life \add is\add* vain. 
\v 17 For what is man, that you have magnified him? Or that you give heed to him? 
\v 18 Will you visit him till the morning, and judge him till \add the time of\add* rest? 
\v 19 How long do you not let me alone, nor let me go, until I will swallow down my spittle? 
\v 20 If I have sinned, what will I be able to do, O you who understand the mind of men? Why have you made me as your accuser, and \add why\add* am I a burden to you? 
\v 21 Why have you not forgotten my wickedness, and purged my sin? But now I will depart to the earth; and in the morning, I am no more. 
\c 8 
\p
\v 1 Then Baldad the Sauchite answered, and said, 
\p
\v 2 How long will you speak these things, \add how long will\add* the breath of your mouth \add be\add* abundant in words? 
\v 3 Will the Lord be unjust when he judges; or will he who has made all things pervert justice? 
\v 4 If your sons have sinned before him, he has cast them away because of their transgression. 
\p
\v 5 But be early in prayer to the Lord Almighty. 
\v 6 If you are pure and true, he will listen to your supplication, and will restore to you the habitation of righteousness. 
\v 7 Then though your beginning should be small, yet your end will be unspeakably great. 
\p
\v 8 For ask of the former generation, and search diligently among the race of \add our\add* fathers: 
\v 9 (for we are of yesterday, and know nothing; for our life upon the earth is a shadow:) 
\v 10 will not these teach you, and report \add to you\add*, and bring out words from \add their\add* heart? 
\v 11 Does the rush flourish without water, or will the reed grow up without moisture? 
\v 12 When it is yet on the root, and \add though\add* it has not been cut down, does not any herb wither before it has received moisture? 
\v 13 Thus then will be the end of all who forget the Lord: for the hope of the ungodly will perish. 
\v 14 For his house will be without inhabitants, and his tent will prove a spider's web. 
\v 15 If he should prop up his house, it will not stand: and when he has taken hold of it, it will not remain. 
\v 16 For it is moist under the sun, and his branch will come forth out of his dung-heap. 
\v 17 He lies down upon a gathering of stones, and will live among flints. 
\v 18 If \add God\add* should destroy \add him\add*, his place will deny him. Have you not seen such things, 
\v 19 that such is the overthrow of the ungodly? And out of the earth another will grow. 
\p
\v 20 For the Lord will by no means reject the harmless man; but he will not receive any gift of the ungodly. 
\v 21 But he will fill with laughter the mouth of the sincere, and their lips with thanksgiving. 
\v 22 But their adversaries will clothe themselves with shame; and the habitation of the ungodly will perish. 
\c 9 
\p
\v 1 Then Job answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 I know of a truth that it is so: for how will a mortal man be just before the Lord? 
\v 3 For if he would enter into judgment with him, \add God\add* would not listen to him, so that he should answer to one of his charges of a thousand. 
\v 4 For he is wise in mind, and mighty, and great: who has hardened himself against him and endured? 
\v 5 Who wears out the mountains, and \add men\add* don't know it: who overturns them in anger. 
\v 6 Who shakes the \add earth\add* under heaven from its foundations, and its pillars totter. 
\v 7 Who commands the sun, and it doesn't rise; and he seals up the stars. 
\v 8 Who alone has stretched out the heavens, and walks on the sea as on firm ground. 
\v 9 Who makes Pleias, and Hesperus, and Arcturus, and the chambers of the south. 
\v 10 Who does great and unsearchable things; glorious also and excellent things, innumerable. 
\p
\v 11 If ever he should go beyond me, I will not see him: if he should pass by me, neither thus have I known \add it\add*. 
\v 12 If he would take away, who will turn him back? Or who will say to him, What have you done? 
\v 13 For \add if\add* he has turned away \add his\add* anger, the monstrous sea-creatures under heaven have stooped under him. 
\p
\v 14 Oh then that he would listen to me, or judge my cause. 
\v 15 For though I be righteous, he will not listen to me: I will entreat his judgment. 
\v 16 If I should call and he should not listen, I cannot believe that he has listened to my voice. 
\p
\v 17 Don't let him crush me with a dark storm: but he has made my bruises many without cause. 
\v 18 For he doesn't allow me to catch my breath, but he has filled me with bitterness. 
\v 19 For indeed he is strong in power: who then will resist his judgment? 
\v 20 For though I should seem righteous, my mouth will be profane: and though I should seem blameless, I will be proved perverse. 
\v 21 For even if I have sinned, I don't know it \add in\add* my soul: but my life is taken away. 
\p
\v 22 So I said, Wrath slays the great and mighty man. 
\v 23 For the worthless die, but the righteous are laughed to scorn. 
\v 24 For they are delivered into the hands of the unrighteous \add man\add*: he covers the faces of the judges \add of the earth\add*: but if it is not he, then who is it? 
\v 25 But my life is swifter than a runner: \add my days\add* have fled away, and they didn't know it. 
\v 26 Or again, is there a trace of \add their\add* path \add left\add* by ships? Or is there one of the flying eagle as it seeks \add its\add* prey? 
\v 27 If I should say, I will forget to speak, I will bow down my face and groan; 
\v 28 I quake in all my limbs, for I know that you will not leave me alone \add as\add* innocent. 
\p
\v 29 But since I am ungodly, why have I not died? 
\v 30 For if I should wash myself with snow, and purge myself with pure hands, 
\v 31 you had thoroughly plunged me in filth, and my garment had abhorred me. 
\v 32 For you are not man like me, with whom I could contend, that we might come together to judgment. 
\v 33 Would that \add he\add* our mediator were \add present\add*, and a reprover, and one who should hear \add the cause\add* between both. 
\v 34 Let him remove \add his\add* rod from me, and don't let his fear terrify me: 
\v 35 so I will not be afraid, but I will speak: for I am not thus conscious \add of guilt\add*. 
\c 10 
\p
\v 1 Weary in my soul, I will pour my words with groans upon him: I will speak, pressed by the bitterness of my soul. 
\v 2 I will say to the Lord, Do not teach me to be impious; and why have you thus judged me? 
\v 3 Is it good before you if I be unrighteous? For you have disowned the work of your hands, and attended to the counsel of the ungodly. 
\v 4 Or do you see as a mortal sees? Or will you look as a man sees? 
\v 5 Or is your life human, or your years \add the years\add* of a man, 
\v 6 that you have enquired into my wickedness, and searched out my sins? 
\v 7 For you know that I have not committed wickedness: but who is he who can deliver out of your hands? 
\p
\v 8 Your hands have formed me and made me; afterwards you changed \add your mind\add*, and struck me. 
\v 9 Remember that you have made me \add as\add* clay, and you turn me again to earth. 
\v 10 Have you not poured me out like milk, and curdled me like cheese? 
\v 11 You clothed me with skin and flesh, and framed me with bones and sinews. 
\v 12 You placed life and mercy with me, and your oversight has preserved my spirit. 
\v 13 Having these things in yourself, I know that you can do all things; for nothing is impossible with you. 
\p
\v 14 If I should sin, you watch me; and you have not cleared me from wickedness. 
\v 15 Or if I should be ungodly, woe is me: and if I should be righteous, I cannot lift myself up, for I am full of dishonour. 
\v 16 For I am hunted like a lion for slaughter; for again you have changed and are terribly destroying me; 
\v 17 renewing against me my torture: and you have dealt with me in great anger, and you have brought trials upon me. 
\p
\v 18 Why then you brought me out of the womb? And why did I not die, and no eye see me, 
\v 19 and I become as if I had not been? For why was I not carried from the womb to the grave? 
\v 20 Isn't the time of my life short? Suffer me to rest a little, 
\v 21 before I go from where I will not return, to a land of darkness and gloominess; 
\v 22 to a land of perpetual darkness, where there is no light, neither \add can any one\add* see the life of mortals. 
\c 11 
\p
\v 1 Then Sophar the Minæan answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 He who speaks much, should also hear on the other side: or does the fluent speaker think himself to be righteous? Blessed \add is\add* the short-lived offspring of woman. 
\v 3 Don't be a speaker of many words; for is there none to answer you? 
\v 4 For don't say, I am pure in my works, and blameless before him. 
\p
\v 5 But oh that the Lord would speak to you, and open his lips with you! 
\v 6 Then he will declare to you the power of wisdom; for it will be double of that which is with you: and then you will know, that a just recompence of your sins has come to you from the Lord. 
\p
\v 7 Can you trace out the ways of the Lord? Or have you come to the end \add of that\add* which the Almighty has made? 
\v 8 Heaven \add is\add* high; and what will you do? And there are deeper things than those in hades; what do you know? 
\v 9 Or longer than the measure of the earth, or the breadth of the sea. 
\p
\v 10 If he should overthrow all things, who will say to him, What have you done? 
\v 11 For he knows the works of transgressors; and when he sees wickedness, he will not overlook \add it\add*. 
\p
\v 12 But man vainly buoys himself up with words; and a mortal born of woman \add is\add* like an ass of the desert. 
\p
\v 13 For if you have made your heart pure, and lift up \add your\add* hands towards him; 
\v 14 if there is any wickedness in your hands, put it far from you, and don't let unrighteousness lodge in your habitation. 
\v 15 For thus will your face shine as pure water; and you will take off uncleanness, and will not fear. 
\v 16 You will forget trouble, as a wave that has passed by; and you will not be scared. 
\v 17 Your prayer \add will be\add* as the morning star, and life will arise to you \add as\add* from the noon-day. 
\v 18 You will be confident, because you have hope; and peace will dawn to you from out of anxiety and care. 
\v 19 For you will be at ease, and there will be no one to fight against you; and many will charge, and make supplication to you. 
\v 20 But safety will fail them; for their hope is destruction, and the eyes of the ungodly will waste away. 
\c 12 
\p
\v 1 Job answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 So then you \add alone\add* are men, and wisdom will die with you? 
\v 3 \add But\add* I also have a heart as well as you. 
\v 4 For a righteous and blameless man has become a subject for mockery. 
\v 5 For it had been ordained that he should fall under others at the appointed time, and that his houses should be plundered by transgressors: yet let no one trust that, being evil, he will be \add held\add* guiltless, 
\v 6 even as many as provoke the Lord, as if there would indeed be no reckoning for them. 
\p
\v 7 But ask now the beasts, if they may speak to you; and the birds of the air, if they may declare to you. 
\v 8 Tell the earth, if it may speak to you: and the fish of the sea will explain to you. 
\v 9 Who then has not known in all these things, that the hand of the Lord has made them? 
\v 10 Because the life of all living things is in his hand, and the breath of every man. 
\p
\v 11 For the ear tries words, and the palate tastes food. 
\v 12 In length of time is wisdom, and in long life knowledge. 
\v 13 With him are wisdom and power, with him counsel and understanding. 
\v 14 If he should cast down, who will build up? If he should shut up against men, who will open? 
\v 15 If he should withhold the water, he will dry the earth: and if he should let it loose, he overthrows and destroys it. 
\v 16 With him are strength and power: he has knowledge and understanding. 
\v 17 He leads counsellors away captive, and confounds the judges of the earth. 
\v 18 He seats kings upon thrones, and girds their loins with a girdle. 
\v 19 He sends away priests into captivity, and overthrows the mighty ones of the earth. 
\v 20 He changes the lips of the trusty, and he knows the understanding of the elders. 
\v 21 He pours dishonour upon princes, and heals the lowly. 
\v 22 Revealing deep things out of darkness: and he has brought into light the shadow of death. 
\v 23 Causing the nations to wander, and destroying them: overthrowing the nations, and leading them \add away\add*. 
\v 24 Perplexing the minds of the princes of the earth: and he causes them to wander in a way they have not known, \add saying\add*, 
\v 25 Let them grope \add in\add* darkness, and \add let there be\add* no light, and let them wander as a drunk man. 
\c 13 
\p
\v 1 Behold, my eye has seen these things, and my ear has heard \add them\add*. 
\v 2 I know all that you too know; and I have not less understanding than you. 
\p
\v 3 Nevertheless I will speak to the Lord, and I will reason before him, if he will. 
\v 4 But you are all bad physicians, and healers of diseases. 
\v 5 But would that you were silent, and it would be wisdom to you in the end. 
\p
\v 6 But hear the reasoning of my mouth, and pay attention to the judgment of my lips. 
\v 7 Do you not speak before the Lord, and utter deceit before him? 
\v 8 Or will you draw back? No, do you yourselves be judges. 
\v 9 For \add it were\add* well if he would thoroughly search you: for though doing all things \add in your power\add* you should attach yourselves to him, 
\v 10 he will not reprove you any less: but if moreover you should secretly respect persons, 
\v 11 will not his whirlpool sweep you round, and terror from him fall upon you? 
\v 12 Your glorying will prove in the end to you like ashes, and your body \add like a body\add* of clay. 
\p
\v 13 Be silent, that I may speak, and cease from \add my\add* anger, 
\v 14 while I may take my flesh in my teeth, and put my life in my hand. 
\v 15 Though the Mighty One should lay hand upon me, because he has begun, truly I will speak, and plead before him. 
\v 16 This will turn to me for salvation; for fraud will have no entrance before him. 
\v 17 Hear, hear my words, for I will declare in your hearing. 
\v 18 Behold, I am near my judgment: I know that I will appear evidently just. 
\v 19 For who is he who will plead with me, that I should now be silent, and expire? 
\p
\v 20 But grant me two things: then I will not hide myself from your face. 
\v 21 Withhold \add your\add* hand from me: and don't let your fear terrify me. 
\v 22 Then you will call, and I will listen to you: or you will speak, and I will give you an answer. 
\v 23 How many are my sins and my transgressions? Teach me what they are. 
\p
\v 24 Why do you hide yourself from me, and deem me your enemy? 
\v 25 Will you be startled \add at me\add*, as \add at\add* a leaf shaken by the wind? Or will you set yourself against me as against grass borne upon the breeze? 
\v 26 For you have written evil things against me, and you have surrounded me with the sins of my youth. 
\v 27 You have placed my foot in the stocks; and you have watched all my works, and have reached even to the roots of my feet.
\v 28 \add I am as\add* that which grows old like a wine-skin, or like a moth-eaten garment. 
\c 14 
\p
\v 1 For a mortal born of a woman \add is\add* short-lived, and full of wrath. 
\v 2 Or he falls like a flower that has bloomed; and he departs like a shadow, and cannot continue. 
\v 3 Have you even taken notice of him, and caused him to enter into judgment before you? 
\v 4 For who will be pure from uncleanness? Not even one; 
\v 5 if even his life should be \add but\add* one day upon the earth: and his months are numbered by him: you have appointed \add him\add* for a time, and he will by no means exceed \add it\add*. 
\p
\v 6 Depart from him, that he may be quiet, and take pleasure in his life, \add though\add* as a hireling. 
\p
\v 7 For there is hope for a tree, even if it should be cut down, \add that\add* it will blossom again, and its branch will not fail. 
\v 8 For though its root should grow old in the earth, and its stem die in the rock; 
\v 9 it will blossom from the scent of water, and will produce a crop, as one newly planted. 
\v 10 But a man who has died is completely gone; and when a mortal has fallen, he is no more. 
\v 11 For even the sea diminishes with time, and a river wastes away and dries up. 
\v 12 Man who has lain down \add in death\add* will certainly not rise again till the heaven be dissolved, and they will not awake from their sleep. 
\p
\v 13 For oh that you had kept me in the grave, and had hidden me until your wrath should cease, and you should set me a time in which you would remember me! 
\v 14 For if a man dies, will he live \add again\add*, having completed all the days of his life? I will endure until I live again. 
\v 15 Then will you call, and I will listen to you: but do not you reject the work of your hands. 
\v 16 But you have numbered my deeds: and not one of my sins will escape you.
\v 17 You have sealed up my transgressions in a bag, and marked if I have been guilty of any transgression unawares. 
\p
\v 18 Truly a mountain falling will completely be destroyed, and a rock will be worn out of its place. 
\v 19 The waters wear the stones, and waters falling headlong \add overflow\add* a heap of the earth: and you destroy the hope of man. 
\v 20 You drive him to an end, and he is gone: you set your face against him, and send him away; 
\v 21 and though his children be multiplied, he doesn't know \add it\add*; and if they be few, he isn't aware. 
\v 22 But his flesh is in pain, and his soul mourns. 
\c 15 
\p
\v 1 Then Eliphaz the Thæmanite answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 Will a wise man give for answer a \add mere\add* breath of wisdom? And does he fill up the pain of his belly, 
\v 3 reasoning with improper sayings, and with words wherein is no profit? 
\v 4 Have you not moreover cast off fear, and accomplished such words before the Lord? 
\v 5 You are guilty by the words of your mouth, neither have you discerned the words of the mighty. 
\v 6 Let your own mouth, and not me, reprove you: and your lips will testify against you. 
\p
\v 7 What! Are you the first man who was born? Or were you established before the hills? 
\v 8 Or have you heard the ordinance of the Lord? Or has God used you as \add his\add* counsellor? And has wisdom come \add only\add* to you? 
\v 9 For what do you know that we don't know? Or what do you understand which we do not also? 
\v 10 Truly among us \add are\add* both the old and very aged man, more advanced in days than your father. 
\v 11 You have been scourged for \add but\add* few of your sins: you have spoken haughtily \add and\add* extravagantly. 
\p
\v 12 What has your heart dared? Or what have your eyes \add aimed at\add*, 
\v 13 that you have vented \add your\add* rage before the Lord, and delivered such words from \add your\add* mouth? 
\v 14 For who, being a mortal, \add is such\add* that he will be blameless? Or, \add who that is\add* born of a woman, that he should be just? 
\v 15 Although he doesn't trust his holy ones; and the heaven isn't pure before him. 
\v 16 Alas then, abominable and unclean is man, drinking unrighteousness as a draught. 
\p
\v 17 But I will tell you, listen to me; I will tell you now what I have seen; 
\v 18 things wise men say, and their fathers have not hidden. 
\v 19 To them alone the earth was given, and no stranger came upon them. 
\v 20 All the life of the ungodly \add is spent\add* in care, and the years granted to the oppressor are numbered. 
\v 21 His terror is in his ears: just when he seems to be at peace, his overthrow will come. 
\v 22 Don't let him trust that he will return from darkness, for he has been already made over to the power of the sword. 
\v 23 He has been appointed to be food for vultures; and he knows within himself that he is doomed to be a carcase: and a dark day will carry him away as with a whirlwind. 
\v 24 Distress also and anguish will come upon him: he will fall as a captain in the first rank. 
\v 25 For he has lifted his hands against the Lord, and he has hardened his neck against the Almighty Lord. 
\v 26 He has run against him with insolence, on the thickness of the back of his shield. 
\v 27 For he has covered his face with his fat, and wrapped his thighs in it.
\v 28 Let him lodge in desolate cities, and enter into houses without inhabitant: and what they have prepared, others will carry away. 
\p
\v 29 Neither he will at all grow rich, nor will his substance remain: he will not cast a shadow upon the earth. 
\v 30 Surely he will not escape the darkness: let the wind blast his blossom, and let his flower fall off. 
\v 31 Don't let him think that he will endure; for his end will be vanity. 
\v 32 His harvest will perish before the time, and his branch will not flourish. 
\v 33 Let him be gathered as the unripe grape before the time, and let him fall as the blossom of the olive. 
\v 34 For death is the witness of an ungodly man, and fire will burn the houses of those who receive gifts. 
\v 35 He will conceive sorrows, and his end will be vanity, and his belly will bear deceit. 
\c 16 
\p
\v 1 But Job answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 I have heard many such things: poor comforters are you all. 
\v 3 What! Is there any reason in vain words? Or what will hinder you from answering? 
\v 4 I also will speak as you \add do\add*: if indeed your soul were in my place, then I would insult you with words, and I would shake my head at you. 
\v 5 If only there were strength in my mouth, and I would not restrain my lips. 
\p
\v 6 For if I should speak, I will not feel the pain of my wound: and if I should be silent, how will I be wounded the less? 
\v 7 But now he has made me weary, and a worn-out fool; and you have laid hold of me. 
\v 8 My falsehood has become a testimony, and has risen up against me: it has confronted me to my face. 
\p
\v 9 In his anger he has cast me down; he has gnashed his teeth upon me: the weapons of his robbers have fallen upon me. 
\v 10 He has attacked me with the keen glances of his eyes; with his sharp \add spear\add* he has struck me \add down\add* upon my knees; and they have run upon me with one accord. 
\p
\v 11 For the Lord has delivered me into the hands of unrighteous men, and thrown me upon the ungodly. 
\v 12 When I was at peace he distracted me: he took me by the hair of the head, and plucked it out: he set me up as a mark. 
\v 13 They surrounded me with spears, aiming at my kidneys: without sparing \add me\add* they poured out my gall upon the ground. 
\v 14 They overthrew me with fall upon fall: they ran upon me in \add their\add* might. 
\v 15 They sewed sackcloth upon my skin, and my strength has been spent on the ground. 
\v 16 My belly has been parched with wailing, and darkness is on my eyelids. 
\v 17 Yet there was no injustice in my hands, and my prayer is pure. 
\p
\v 18 Earth, don't cover over the blood of my flesh, and let my cry have no place. 
\v 19 Now, behold, my witness is in heaven, and my advocate is on high. 
\v 20 Let my supplication come to the Lord, and let my eye weep before him. 
\v 21 Oh that a man might plead before the Lord, even \add as\add* the son of man with his neighbour! 
\v 22 But my years are numbered and \add their end\add* come, and I will go by the way by which I will not return. 
\c 17 
\p
\v 1 I perish, carried away by the wind, and I seek for burial, and don't obtain \add it\add*. 
\v 2 Weary, I entreat; and what have I done? And strangers have stolen my goods. 
\v 3 Who is this? Let him join hands with me. 
\v 4 For you have hid their heart from wisdom; therefore you will not exalt them. 
\v 5 He will promise mischief to \add his\add* companions: but \add their\add* eyes have failed for \add their\add* children. 
\p
\v 6 But you have made me a byword among the nations, and I have become a scorn to them. 
\v 7 For my eyes are dimmed through pain; I have been grievously beset by all. 
\v 8 Wonder has seized true men upon this; and let the just rise up against the transgressor. 
\v 9 But let the faithful hold on his own way, and let him that is pure of hands take courage. 
\v 10 Nevertheless, all of you strengthen \add yourselves\add* and come now, for I do not find truth in you. 
\p
\v 11 My days have passed in groaning, and my heart-strings are broken. 
\v 12 I have turned the night into day: the light is short because of darkness. 
\v 13 For if I remain, Hades is my habitation: and my bed has been made in darkness. 
\v 14 I have called upon death to be my father, and corruption \add to be\add* my mother and sister. 
\v 15 Where then is yet my hope? Or \add where\add* will I see my good? 
\v 16 Will they go down with me to Hades, or will we go down together to the tomb? 
\c 18 
\p
\v 1 Then Baldad the Sauchite answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 How long will you go on? Pause, that we also may speak. 
\v 3 For why have we been silent before you like animals? 
\v 4 Anger has possessed you: for what if you should die; would \add the earth\add* under heaven be desolate? Or will the mountains be overthrown from their foundations? 
\p
\v 5 But the light of the ungodly will be quenched, and their flame will not go up. 
\v 6 His light \add will be\add* darkness in \add his\add* habitation, and his lamp will be put out with him. 
\v 7 Let the lowliest men prey on his possessions, and let his own counsel deceive \add him\add*. 
\v 8 His foot also has been caught in a snare, \add and\add* let it be entangled in a net. 
\v 9 Let snares come upon him: he will strengthen those that thirst for his destruction. 
\v 10 His snare is hidden in the earth, and that which will take him is by the path. 
\v 11 Let pains destroy him all around, and let many \add enemies\add* come about him, 
\v 12 \add vex him\add* with distressing hunger: and a terrible downfall has been prepared for him. 
\v 13 Let the soles of his feet be devoured: and death will consume his beauty. 
\v 14 Let health be completely banished from his tabernacle, and let distress seize upon him with a charge from the king. 
\v 15 It will dwell in his tent in his night: his excellency will be sown with brimstone. 
\v 16 His roots will be dried up from beneath, and his crop will fall away from above. 
\v 17 Let his memorial perish out of the earth, and his name will be publicly cast out. 
\v 18 Let \add one\add* drive him from light into darkness. 
\v 19 He will not be known among his people, nor his house preserved on the earth. 
\v 20 But strangers will dwell in his possessions: the last groaned for him, and astonishment seized the first. 
\p
\v 21 These are the houses of the unrighteous, and this is the place of those who don't know the Lord. 
\c 19 
\p
\v 1 Then Job answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 How long will you vex my soul, and destroy me with words? Only know that the Lord has dealt with me thus. 
\v 3 You speak against me; you do not feel for me, but bear hard upon me. 
\v 4 Yes truly, I have erred in truth, (but the error abides with myself) 
\v 4a in having spoken words which it was not right \add to speak\add*; and my words err, and are unseasonable. 
\v 5 But alas! For you magnify yourselves against me, and insult me with reproach. 
\v 6 Know then that it is the Lord who has troubled \add me\add*, and has raised his bulwark against me. 
\v 7 Behold, I laugh at reproach; I will not speak: I will cry out, but there is no justice anywhere.
\v 8 I am hemmed in on every side, and can surely not escape: he has set darkness before my face. 
\v 9 He has stripped me of my glory, and has taken the crown from my head. 
\v 10 He has torn me all around, and I am gone: and he has cut off my hope like a tree. 
\v 11 He has dreadfully handled me in anger, and has counted me for an enemy. 
\v 12 His forces also came upon me with one accord, those who lie in wait surrounded me on my paths. 
\p
\v 13 My brothers have stood aloof from me; they have regarded strangers \add rather\add* than me: and my friends have become pitiless. 
\v 14 My nearest of kin have not acknowledged me, and those who knew my name, have forgotten me. 
\v 15 My household and my maid-servants regard me as a stranger.
\v 16 I called my servant, and he didn't listen; I pleaded with him.
\v 17 I pleaded with my wife, and coaxed the sons of my concubines. 
\v 18 But they rejected me forever; whenever I rise up, they speak against me. 
\v 19 Those who saw me abhorred me: the very persons whom I had loved, rose up against me. 
\v 20 My flesh is corrupt under my skin, and my bones are held in \add my\add* teeth. 
\v 21 Pity me, pity me, O friends; for it is the hand of the Lord who has touched me. 
\v 22 Why do you persecute me as also the Lord \add does\add*, and are not satisfied with my flesh? 
\p
\v 23 For oh that my words were written, and that they were recorded in a book forever, 
\v 24 with an iron pen and lead, or graven in the rocks! 
\v 25 For I know that the one who will deliver me is everlasting, 
\v 26 \add and\add* to raise up this skin of mine that endures these things: for these things have been accomplished for me by the Lord; 
\v 27 which I myself know, which my eye has seen and not another. All this has been accomplished within me.
\p
\v 28 But if you will also say, What will we say before him, and \add so\add* find the root of the matter in him? 
\v 29 You also beware of concealment, for wrath will come upon the lawless; and then they will know where their wealth is.
\c 20 
\p
\v 1 Then Sophar the Minæan answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 I did not suppose that you would answer thus: neither do you understand more than I. 
\v 3 I will hear my shameful reproach; and the spirit of my understanding answers me. 
\p
\v 4 Have you \add not\add* known these things of old, from the time that man was set upon the earth? 
\v 5 But the mirth of the ungodly is a signal downfall, and the joy of transgressors is destruction: 
\v 6 although his gifts should go up to heaven, and his sacrifice reach the clouds. 
\v 7 For when he will seem to be well established, then he will completely perish: and those who knew him will say, Where is he? 
\v 8 Like a dream that has fled away, he will not be found; and he has fled like a vision of the night. 
\v 9 The eye has looked upon him, but will not \add see him\add* again; and his place will no longer perceive him. 
\v 10 Let \add his\add* inferiors destroy his children, and let his hands kindle the fire of sorrow. 
\v 11 His bones have been filled with \add vigour of\add* his youth, and it will lie down with him in the dust. 
\p
\v 12 Though evil be sweet in his mouth, \add though\add* he will hide it under his tongue; 
\v 13 though he will not spare it, and will not leave it, but holds it in his mouth;
\v 14 yet he will not at all be able to help himself; the \f + \fr 20:14 \fqa Gr. \ft gall.\f*poison of an asp is in his belly. 
\p
\v 15 \add His\add* wealth unjustly collected will be vomited up; a messenger \add of wrath\add* will drag him out of his house. 
\v 16 Let him suck the venom of serpents, and let the serpent's tongue kill him. 
\v 17 Don't let him see the milk of the pastures, nor the supplies of honey and butter. 
\v 18 He has laboured unprofitably and in vain, \add for\add* wealth of which he will not taste: \add it is\add* as a lean thing, unfit for food, which he cannot swallow. 
\v 19 For he has broken down the houses of many helpless: and he has plundered a dwelling he did not build.
\v 20 There is no security to his possessions; he will not be saved by his desire. 
\v 21 There is nothing remaining of his provisions; therefore his goods will not flourish. 
\v 22 But just when he seems to be fully satisfied, he will be distressed; and every hardship will come upon him. 
\p
\v 23 If by any means he would fill his belly, let \add God\add* send upon him the fury of wrath; let him bring a torrent of pains upon him. 
\v 24 He will by no means escape from the power of the sword; let the bronze bow wound him. 
\v 25 Let the arrow pierce through his body; and let the stars be against his dwelling-place: let terrors come upon him. 
\v 26 Let all darkness await him: an unquenchable fire will consume him; and let a stranger afflict his house. 
\v 27 Let the heaven reveal his iniquities, and the earth rise up against him. 
\v 28 Let destruction bring his house to an end; let a day of wrath come upon him. 
\v 29 This is the portion of an ungodly man from the Lord, and the inheritance of his possessions from the Overseer.
\c 21 
\p
\v 1 But Job answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 Hear, hear my words, that I may not have this consolation from you. 
\v 3 Raise me, and I will speak; then you will not laugh me to scorn. 
\v 4 What! Is my reproof from man? And why should I not be angry? 
\v 5 Look upon me, and wonder, laying your hand upon your cheek. 
\p
\v 6 For even when I remember, I am alarmed, and pains seize my flesh. 
\v 7 Why do the ungodly live, grow old, and increase in prosperity? 
\v 8 Their offspring are according to their desire, and their children are before their eyes.
\v 9 Their houses prosper, and fear is nowhere \add among them\add*, there is no scourge from the Lord upon them. 
\v 10 Their cow does not cast her calf, and their \add beast\add* with young is safe, and does not miscarry. 
\v 11 They continue like eternal sheep, and their children play before \add them\add*, 
\v 12 taking up the psaltery and harp; and they rejoice at the voice of a song. 
\v 13 They spend their days in prosperity, and fall asleep in the rest of the Hades. 
\v 14 Yet \add such a man\add* says to the Lord, Depart from me; I don't want to know your ways. 
\v 15 What is the Mighty One, that we should serve him? And what profit is there that we should approach him? 
\p
\v 16 For their wealth was in \add their\add* hands, but he doesn't regard the works of the ungodly. 
\v 17 Nevertheless, the lamp of the ungodly also will be put out, and destruction will come upon them, and pangs of vengeance will seize them. 
\v 18 They will be as chaff before the wind, or as dust which the storm has taken up. 
\v 19 Let his possessions fail \add to supply\add* his children: \add God\add* will repay him, and he will know it. 
\v 20 Let his eyes see his own destruction, and don't let him be saved by the Lord. 
\v 21 For what concern has he for his house after him, when the number of his months has been apportioned?
\p
\v 22 Is it not the Lord who teaches understanding and knowledge? He himself judges bloodshed.
\v 23 One will die in his full strength, and wholly at ease and prosperous; 
\v 24 and his inward parts are full of fat, and his marrow flows freely.
\v 25 Another dies in bitterness of soul, not eating any good thing. 
\v 26 But they lie down in the earth together, and decay covers them. 
\p
\v 27 So I know you, that you presumptuously attack me: 
\v 28 so that you will say, Where is the house of the prince? And where is the covering of the tents of the ungodly? 
\v 29 Ask travellers on the road, and do not reject their testimony. 
\v 30 For the wicked hastens to the day of destruction: they will be led away for the day of his vengeance. 
\v 31 Who will tell him his way to his face, because he has done \add it\add*? Who will recompense him? 
\v 32 He has been led away to the tombs, and he has watched over the burial mound. 
\v 33 The stones of the stream bed have been sweet to him, and every man will depart after him, and countless others went before him. 
\v 34 How then do you comfort me in vain? Because I have no rest from your molestation. 
\c 22 
\p
\v 1 Then Eliphaz the Thæmanite answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 Is it not the Lord who teaches understanding and knowledge? 
\v 3 For what does it matter to the Lord if you were blameless in \add your\add* works? Or is it profitable that you should perfect your way? 
\v 4 Will you maintain and plead your own cause? And will he enter into judgment with you? 
\p
\v 5 Isn't your wickedness abundant, and your sins innumerable? 
\v 6 You have taken security from your brothers for nothing, and have taken away the clothing of the naked. 
\v 7 Neither have you given water to the thirsty to drink, but have taken away the morsel of the hungry. 
\v 8 You have shown favour to some and established them on the earth,
\v 9 but you have sent widows away empty, and have afflicted orphans. 
\v 10 Therefore snares have surrounded you, and terrible turmoil overwhelms you. 
\v 11 The light has proved darkness to you, and water has covered you on your lying down. 
\p
\v 12 Does not he who dwells in the high places observe? And has he not brought down the proud? 
\v 13 You have said, What does the Mighty One know? Does he judge in the dark? 
\v 14 A cloud is his hiding-place, and he will not be seen; and he passes through the circle of heaven. 
\v 15 Will you \add not\add* mark the old way, which righteous men have trodden? 
\v 16 They were seized before their time: their foundations \add are as\add* an overflowing stream. 
\v 17 Who say, What will the Lord do to us? Or what will the Almighty bring upon us? 
\v 18 Yet he filled their houses with good things: but the counsel of the wicked is far from him. 
\v 19 The righteous have seen \add it\add*, and laughed, and the blameless one has derided \add them\add*. 
\v 20 Truly their substance has been completely destroyed, and the fire will devour what is left of their \add property\add*. 
\p
\v 21 Stand firm, if you can endure; then your fruit will prosper. 
\v 22 Receive a declaration from his mouth, and lay up his words in your heart. 
\v 23 If you will turn and humble yourself before the Lord, you have \add thus\add* removed unrighteousness far from your habitation. 
\v 24 You will lay up for yourself \add treasure\add* in a heap on the rock; and Sophir \add will be\add* as the rock of the torrent. 
\v 25 So the Almighty will be your helper from enemies, and he will bring you forth pure as silver that has been tried by fire. 
\v 26 Then you will have boldness before the Lord, looking up cheerfully to heaven. 
\v 27 He will hear you when you pray to him, and he will grant you \add power\add* to pay your vows. 
\v 28 He will establish to you again a habitation of righteousness and there will be light upon your paths. 
\v 29 Because you have humbled yourself; and you will say, \add Man\add* has behaved proudly, but he will save him that is of lowly eyes. 
\v 30 He will deliver the innocent, you will be saved by your pure hands. 
\c 23 
\p
\v 1 Then Job answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 Yes, I know that my vindication is from my own hand; and his hand has become heavy on my groaning. 
\v 3 Who then would know whether I might find him, and come to an end?
\v 4 I would plead my own cause, and he would fill my mouth with arguments. 
\v 5 I would know the remedies which he would speak to me, and I would perceive what he would tell me. 
\v 6 Though he should come on me in great strength, he would not use threats against me; 
\v 7 for truth and reproof are from him; and he would bring forth my judgment to an end. 
\v 8 For I go to what is before, and I am no more; and of what comes after, but what do I know?
\p
\v 9 When he acts on the left, I do not perceive \add him\add*: when he envelops \add me\add* on the right, I do not see \add him\add*. 
\v 10 For he already knows my way; and he has tested me like gold. 
\v 11 I will go forth according to his commandments, for I have kept his ways; and I will not turn aside from his commandments, 
\v 12 neither I will transgress; but I have hid his words in my bosom. 
\p
\v 13 But if even he has decided thus, who can oppose him? For what he himself willed, he also did.
\v 15 Therefore I am troubled at him; and when I was reproved, I thought of him. 
\v 15a Therefore let me take good heed before him: I will consider, and be afraid of him. 
\p
\v 16 But the Lord has made my heart faint, and the Almighty has terrified me. 
\v 17 For I didn't know that darkness would come upon me, and thick darkness has covered my face. 
\c 24 
\p
\v 1 But why are times hidden from the Lord, 
\v 2 while the ungodly have crossed the boundary, carrying off the sheep along with the shepherd? 
\v 3 They have seized the donkey of the fatherless, and taken the widow's ox for a pledge. 
\p
\v 4 They have turned aside the weak from the right way: and the meek of the earth have hidden themselves together. 
\v 5 They go out like donkeys in the field, going out for my sake to their labour; bread is sweet to their little ones.
\p
\v 6 They have reaped a field that was not their own before the time: the poor have laboured in the vineyards of the ungodly without pay and without food. 
\v 7 They have caused many naked to sleep without clothes, and they have taken away the covering of their body. 
\v 8 They are wet with the drops of the mountains: they have embraced the rock, because they had no shelter. 
\p
\v 9 They have snatched the fatherless from the breast, and have afflicted the outcast. 
\v 10 They have wrongfully caused \add others\add* to sleep without clothing, and taken away the morsel of the hungry. 
\p
\v 11 They have unrighteously laid wait in narrow places, and have not known the righteous way. 
\v 12 Who have cast forth \add the\add* poor from the city and their own houses, and the soul of the children has groaned aloud. 
\p
\v 13 Why then has he not visited these? Because they were upon the earth, and took no notice, and they didn't know the way of righteousness, neither have they walked in their \add appointed\add* paths? 
\v 14 But having known their works, he delivered them into darkness: and in the night one will be as a thief: 
\v 15 and the eye of the adulterer has watched \add for\add* the darkness, saying, Eye will not perceive me, and he puts a covering on his face. 
\v 16 In darkness he digs through houses: by day they conceal themselves securely: they don't know the light. 
\v 17 For the morning is to them all \add as\add* the shadow of death, for \add each\add* will be conscious of the terror of the shadow of death. 
\v 18 He is swift on the face of the water: let his portion be cursed on the earth; and let their plants be laid bare. 
\v 19 \add Let them be\add* withered upon the earth; for they have plundered the sheaves of the fatherless. 
\p
\v 20 Then his sin is brought to remembrance, and he vanishes like a vapour of dew: but let what he has done be recompensed to him, and let every unrighteous one be crushed like rotten wood. 
\p
\v 21 For he has not treated the barren woman well, and has had no pity on a feeble woman. 
\v 22 In wrath he has overthrown the helpless: therefore when he has arisen, \add a man\add* will not feel secure of his own life. 
\v 23 When he has fallen sick, don't let him hope to recover: but let him perish by disease. 
\v 24 For his exaltation has hurt many; but he has withered like a mallow plant in the heat, or as an ear of grain falling off from the stalk by itself. 
\v 25 But if not, who is he who says I speak falsely, and counts my words as nothing?
\c 25 
\p
\v 1 Then Baldad the Sauchite answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 What prelude or fear is his—even he who makes all things in the highest? 
\v 3 For let none think that there is a respite for robbers: and upon whom will there not come a snare from him? 
\v 4 For how will a mortal be just before the Lord? Or who that is born of a woman will purify himself? 
\v 5 If he gives an order to the moon, then it doesn't shine; and the stars are not pure before him. 
\v 6 But alas! Man is corruption, and the son of man a worm. 
\c 26 
\p
\v 1 But Job answered and said, 
\p
\v 2 To whom do you attach yourself, or whom are you going to assist? Is it not he who \add has\add* much strength, and \add he\add* who has a strong arm? 
\v 3 To whom have you given counsel? Is it not to him who has all wisdom? Whom will you follow? Is it not one who has the greatest power? 
\v 4 To whom have you uttered words? And whose breath is it that has come forth from you? 
\p
\v 5 Will the mighty ones be born from under the waters and their neighbours? 
\v 6 Hades is naked before him, and destruction has no covering. 
\v 7 He stretches out the north over nothing, and hangs the earth on nothing; 
\v 8 binding water in his clouds, and the cloud isn't torn under it. 
\v 9 He keeps back the face of his throne, stretching out his cloud upon it. 
\v 10 He has encompassed the face of the water by an appointed ordinance, until the end of light and darkness. 
\v 11 The pillars of heaven are prostrate and astonished at his rebuke. 
\v 12 He has calmed the sea with \add his\add* might, and by \add his\add* wisdom the monstrous sea-creature has been wounded. 
\v 13 The barriers of heaven fear him, and by a command he has killed the rebel dragon. 
\v 14 Behold, these are parts of his way; and we will listen to him at the least intimation of his word: but who knows the strength of his thunder, when he will employ \add it\add*? 
\c 27 
\p
\v 1 Job further continued and said in his parable, 
\p
\v 2 \add As\add* God lives, who has thus judged me; and the Almighty, who has embittered my soul; 
\v 3 truly, while my breath is yet in \add me\add*, and the breath of God which remains to me is in my nostrils, 
\v 4 my lips will not speak evil words, neither will my soul meditate unrighteous thoughts. 
\v 5 Far be it from me that I should justify you till I die; for I will not let go my innocence, 
\v 6 but keeping fast to \add my\add* righteousness I will by no means let it go: for I am not aware of having done any thing wrong.
\v 7 No rather, but let my enemies be as the overthrow of the ungodly, and those who rise up against me, as the destruction of transgressors. 
\p
\v 8 For what is the hope of the ungodly, that he holds to it? Will he indeed trust in the Lord \add and\add* be saved? 
\v 9 Will God hear his prayer? Or, when distress has come upon him, 
\v 10 does he have any confidence before him? Or will \add God\add* hear him as he calls upon him? 
\p
\v 11 Yet now I will tell you what is in the hand of the Lord: I will not lie concerning the things which are with the Almighty. 
\v 12 Behold, you all know that you are adding vanity to vanity. 
\v 13 This is the portion of an ungodly man from the Lord, and the possession of oppressors will come upon them from the Almighty. 
\v 14 If their children be many, they will be for slaughter: and if they grow up, they will beg. 
\v 15 Those who survive him will end in death, and no one will pity their widows. 
\v 16 Even if he should gather silver as earth, and prepare gold as clay; 
\v 17 the righteous will gain all these things, and the truehearted will possess his wealth. 
\v 18 His house is gone like moths, and like a spider's web. 
\v 19 The rich man lied down, and will not continue: he opens his eyes, and he is not. 
\v 20 Pains have come upon him as water, and darkness has carried him away by night. 
\v 21 A burning wind will catch him, and he will depart, and it will completely drive him out of his place. 
\v 22 And he will cast upon him without sparing; he will flee from his hand.
\v 23 He will cause \add men\add* to clap their hands against them, and will hiss him out of his place. 
\c 28 
\p
\v 1 For there is a place for the silver, where it comes, and a place for the gold, where it is refined. 
\v 2 For iron comes out of the earth, and brass is hewn out like stone. 
\p
\v 3 He has set a bound to darkness, and he searches out every limit: a stone, darkness, and the shadow of death. 
\v 4 There is a cutting off of the torrent because of dust: so those who forget the right way are weakened; they are removed from \add among\add* men. 
\v 5 \add As for\add* the earth, out of it will come bread: under it has been turned up as it were fire. 
\v 6 Her stones are the place of the sapphire: and \add her\add* dust \add supplies\add* man with gold. 
\v 7 \add There is\add* a path that no bird has known, neither has the eye of the vulture seen it: 
\v 8 neither have the sons of the proud trodden it, a lion has not passed upon it. 
\v 9 He has stretched forth his hand on the sharp \add rock\add*, and turned up mountains by the roots: 
\v 10 and he has interrupted the whirlpools of rivers, and my eye has seen every precious thing. 
\v 11 He has laid bare the depths of rivers, and has brought his power to light. 
\p
\v 12 But where has wisdom been uncovered? And what is the place of knowledge? 
\v 13 No mortal has known its way, nor will it be found among men. 
\v 14 The depth said, It isn't in me: and the sea said, It isn't with me. 
\v 15 One will not give fine gold instead of it, neither will silver be weighed in exchange for it. 
\v 16 Neither will it be compared with gold of Sophir, with the precious onyx and sapphire. 
\v 17 Gold and crystal will not be equalled to it, neither will vessels of gold be its exchange. 
\v 18 Coral and fine pearl will not be mentioned: but seek wisdom above the most precious things. 
\v 19 The topaz of Ethiopia will not be equalled to it; it will not be compared with pure gold. 
\p
\v 20 Where then is wisdom found? And what is the place of understanding? 
\v 21 It has escaped the notice of every man, and has been hidden from the birds of the sky. 
\v 22 Destruction and Death said, We have heard the report of it. 
\p
\v 23 God has well ordered the way of it, and he knows the place of it. 
\v 24 For he surveys the whole \add earth\add* under heaven, knowing the things in the earth: 
\v 25 all that he has made; the weight of the winds, the measures of the water. 
\v 26 When he made \add them\add*, thus he saw and numbered them, and made a way for the pealing of the thunder. 
\v 27 Then he saw it, and declared it: he prepared it \add and\add* traced it out. 
\v 28 He said to man, Behold, godliness is wisdom; and to abstain from evil is understanding. 
\c 29 
\p
\v 1 Job continued and said in his parable, 
\p
\v 2 Oh that I were as in months past, when God preserved me! 
\v 3 As when his lamp shone over my head; when by his light I walked through darkness. 
\v 4 \add As\add* when I steadfastly pursued my ways, when God took care of my house. 
\v 5 When I was very fruitful, and my children were about me; 
\v 6 when my ways were moistened with butter, and the mountains flowed for me with milk. 
\p
\v 7 When I went out early in the city, and the seat was placed for me in the streets. 
\v 8 The young men saw me, and hid themselves: and all the old men stood up. 
\v 9 The great men ceased speaking, and laid their finger on their mouth. 
\v 10 Those who heard \add me\add* blessed me, and their tongue clung to their throat. 
\v 11 For the ear heard, and blessed me; and the eye saw me, and turned aside. 
\v 12 For I saved the poor out of the hand of the oppressor, and helped the fatherless who had no helper. 
\v 13 Let the blessing of the perishing one come upon me; yes, the mouth of the widow has blessed me. 
\v 14 Also I put on righteousness, and clothed myself with justice like a mantle. 
\v 15 I was the eye of the blind, and the foot of the lame. 
\v 16 I was the father of the helpless; and I searched out the cause which I didn't know. 
\v 17 I broke the fangs of the unrighteous; I plucked the spoil out of their teeth. 
\v 18 I said, My age will continue as the stem of a palm-tree; I will live a long while. 
\v 19 \add My\add* root was spread out by the water, and the dew would lodge on my crop. 
\v 20 My glory was fresh in me, and my bow prospered in his hand. 
\p
\v 21 \add Men\add* heard me, and gave heed, and they were silent at my counsel. 
\v 22 At my word they didn't speak again, and they were very glad whenever I spoke to them. 
\v 23 As the thirsty earth expecting the rain, so they \add waited for\add* my speech. 
\v 24 Were I to laugh on them, they would not believe \add it\add*; and the light of my face has not failed. 
\v 25 I chose out their way, and sat chief, and dwelt as a king among warriors, as one comforting mourners. 
\c 30 
\p
\v 1 But now the youngest have laughed me to scorn, now they reprove me in \add their\add* turn, whose fathers I despise; whom I did not deem worthy \add to be with\add* my shepherd dogs. 
\v 2 Indeed, what use to me was the strength of their hands? Their fulfilment had perished. 
\v 3 Barren in want and famine, they fled the waterless place only yesterday—distress and misery.
\v 4 Who gathered salt herbs on the sounding waste, whose food was salt herbs, despised and degraded, lacking every good thing, who also chewed the roots of trees because of great hunger.
\p
\v 5 Thieves have risen up against me, 
\v 6 whose houses were the caves of the rocks, who lived under the wild shrubs. 
\v 7 They will cry out among the rustling \add bushes\add*. 
\v 8 \add They are\add* sons of fools and vile men, \add whose\add* name and glory \add are\add* quenched from off the earth. 
\v 9 But now I am their music, and they have me for a by-word. 
\v 10 They stood far off and loathed me and did not hesitate to spit in my face.
\v 11 For he has opened his quiver and afflicted me: they also have cast off the restraint of my presence. 
\v 12 They have risen up against \add me\add* on the right hand of \add their\add* offspring; they have stretched out their foot, and directed against me the ways of their destruction. 
\v 13 My paths are ruined; for they have stripped off my clothing: he has shot at me with his weapons. 
\v 14 He has pleaded against me as he will: I am overwhelmed with pains. 
\v 15 My pains return upon \add me\add*; my hope is gone like the wind, and my safety as a cloud. 
\p
\v 16 Even now my life will be poured out upon me; and days of anguish seize me. 
\v 17 By night my bones burn up; and my sinews are dissolved. 
\v 18 With great force it has taken hold of my garment; it surrounds me like the collar of my tunic. 
\v 19 You have counted me as clay; my portion is in dust and ashes. 
\p
\v 20 I have cried to you, but you don't hear me: but they stood still, and observed me. 
\v 21 They attacked me also without mercy: you have scourged me with a strong hand. 
\v 22 You have put me to grief, and have cast me away from safety. 
\v 23 For I know that death will destroy me: for the earth is the house \add appointed\add* for every mortal. 
\v 24 Oh then that I might lay hands upon myself, or at least ask another, and he should do this for me. 
\v 25 Yet I wept over every helpless man; I groaned when I saw a man in distress. 
\v 26 But I, when I waited for good things, behold, days of evils came the more upon me. 
\p
\v 27 My belly boiled, and would not be silent: days of poverty overtook me. 
\v 28 I went mourning without restraint: and I have stood and cried out in the assembly. 
\v 29 I have become a brother of sirens, and a companion of ostriches. 
\v 30 My skin has been greatly blackened, and my bones are burned with heat. 
\v 31 My harp also has been turned into mourning, and my song into my weeping. 
\c 31 
\p
\v 1 I made a covenant with my eyes, and I will not think upon a virgin. 
\v 2 And what portion has God given from above? And what inheritance the Mighty One from the heights? 
\v 3 Alas! Destruction to the unrighteous, and rejection to those who do wickedness. 
\v 4 Will he not see my way, and number all my steps? 
\v 5 But if I had gone with scorners, and if too my foot has hurried to deceit: 
\v 6 (for I am weighed in a just balance, and the Lord knows my innocence:) 
\v 7 if my foot has turned aside out of the way, or if my heart has followed my eye, and if too I have touched gifts with my hands; 
\v 8 then let me sow, and let others eat; and let me be uprooted on the earth. 
\v 9 If my heart has gone forth after another man's wife, and if I laid wait at her doors; 
\v 10 then let my wife also please another, and let my children be brought low. 
\v 11 For the fury of anger is uncontrollable at the defiling of a man’s wife.
\v 12 For it is a fire burning on every side, and whoever it attacks, it completely destroys. 
\p
\v 13 If too I denied justice to my servant or handmaid when they contended with me; 
\v 14 what then will I do if the Lord should try me? And if also he should at all visit me, can I make an answer? 
\v 15 Were not they too formed as I also was formed in the womb? Yes, we were formed in the same womb. 
\p
\v 16 But the helpless did not lack anything they needed, and I did not cause the widow's eye to fail. 
\v 17 If too I ate my morsel alone, and did not impart \add of it\add* to the orphan; 
\v 18 (for I nourished \add them\add* as a father from my youth, and guided \add them\add* from my mother's womb.) 
\v 19 If too I overlooked the naked as he was perishing, and did not clothe him; 
\v 20 and if the poor did not bless me, and their shoulders were \add not\add* warmed with the fleece of my lambs; 
\v 21 if I lifted my hand against an orphan, trusting that my strength was far superior:
\v 22 let then my shoulder be torn from from the collarbone, and my arm be shattered from the elbow. 
\v 23 For the fear of the Lord constrained me, and I cannot bear up because of his burden. 
\p
\v 24 If I made gold my treasure, and if I also trusted the precious stone; 
\v 25 and if I also rejoiced when my wealth was abundant, and if I also laid my hand on innumerable \add treasures\add*: 
\v 26 (do I not see the shining sun eclipsed, and the moon waning? For it is not in their power:) 
\v 27 and if my heart was secretly deceived, and if I raised my hand upon my mouth and kissed it: 
\v 28 let this also then be reckoned to me as the greatest wickedness: for I \add should\add* have lied against the Lord Most High. 
\v 29 If too I was glad at the fall of my enemies, and my heart said, Aha! 
\v 30 let then my ear hear my curse, and let me be a by-word among my people in my affliction. 
\p
\v 31 If too my handmaids have often said, Oh that we might be satisfied with his flesh; (because I was very kind: 
\v 32 for the stranger did not lodge outside, and my door was opened to every traveller:) 
\v 33 or if too having sinned unintentionally, I hid my sin; 
\v 34 (for I did not stand in awe of a great multitude, so as not to declare boldly before them:) and if too I permitted a poor man to go out of my door empty-handed: 
\v 35 (Oh that I had a hearer,) and if I had not feared the hand of the Lord; and \add as to\add* the written charge which I had against any one, 
\v 36 I would place \add it\add* as a garland on my shoulders, and read it aloud. 
\v 37 If I did not tear it up and return it, having taken nothing from the debtor: 
\p
\v 38 If at any time the land groaned against me, and if its furrows wept together; 
\v 39 and if I ate its strength alone without payment, and if too I grieved the life of the owner of the land, by casting him out:
\v 40 then let the nettle come up to me instead of wheat, and a bramble instead of barley. And Job ceased speaking. 
\c 32 
\p
\v 1 His three friends also ceased answering Job: for Job was righteous in their sight.
\p
\v 2 Then Elius the son of Barachiel, the Buzite, of the kindred of Ram, of the country of Ausis, was angered: and he was very angry with Job, because he justified himself before the Lord. 
\v 3 He was also very angry with \add his\add* three friends, because they were not able to return answers to Job, yet set him down for an ungodly man. 
\v 4 But Elius had refrained giving an answer to Job, because they were older than he. 
\v 5 Elius saw that there was no answer in the mouth of the three men; and he was angered in his wrath. 
\v 6 Elius the Buzite the son of Barachiel answered and said, 
\p I am younger in age, and you are elder; so I kept silence, fearing to declare to you my own knowledge. 
\v 7 I said, It isn't time that speaks, though in many years \add men\add* know wisdom: 
\v 8 but there is a spirit in mortals; and the inspiration of the Almighty is that which teaches. 
\v 9 The long-lived are not wise \add as such\add*; neither do the aged know judgment. 
\v 10 So I said, Hear me, and I will tell you what I know. 
\p
\v 11 Listen to my words; for I will speak in your hearing, until you will have tried \add the matter\add* with words: 
\v 12 and I will understand as far as you; and, behold, there was no one of you who answered Job's words in argument, 
\v 13 lest you should say, We have found that we have added wisdom to the Lord. 
\v 14 You have commissioned a man to speak such words. 
\p
\v 15 They were afraid, they answered no longer; they gave up their speaking. 
\v 16 I waited, (for I had not spoken,) because they stood still, they didn't answer. 
\v 17 Elius continued, and said, I will again speak, 
\v 18 for I am full of words, for the spirit of my belly destroys me. 
\v 19 My belly is as a skin of sweet wine bound up \add and\add* ready to burst; or as a brazier's labouring bellows. 
\v 20 I will speak, that I may open my lips and relieve myself. 
\v 21 For truly I will not be awed because of man, nor indeed will I be greatly agitated before a mortal. 
\v 22 For I don't know how to respect persons: and if otherwise, even the moths would eat me. 
\c 33 
\p
\v 1 But now hear my words, Job, and listen to my speech. 
\v 2 For behold, I have opened my mouth, and my tongue has spoken. 
\v 3 My heart \add will be found\add* pure by \add my\add* words; and the understanding of my lips will meditate purity. 
\v 4 The Divine Spirit is that which formed me, and the breath of the Almighty that which teaches me. 
\v 5 If you can, give me an answer: wait therefore; stand against me, and I \add will stand\add* against you. 
\v 6 You are formed out of the clay as also am I: we have been formed out of the same \add substance\add*. 
\v 7 My fear will not terrify you, neither will my hand be heavy upon you. 
\p
\v 8 But you have said in my ears, (I have heard the voice of your words;) because you say, I am pure, not having sinned; 
\v 9 I am blameless, for I have not transgressed. 
\v 10 Yet he has uncovered a charge against me, and he has reckoned me as an adversary. 
\v 11 He has put my foot in the stocks, and has watched all my ways. 
\v 12 For how can you say, I am righteous, yet he has not listened to me? For he who is above mortals is eternal. 
\p
\v 13 But you say, Why has he not heard every word of my cause? 
\v 14 For when the Lord speaks once, or a second time, 
\v 15 \add sending\add* a dream, or in the meditation of the night; (as when a dreadful alarm happens to fall upon men, in slumberings on the bed:) 
\v 16 then he opens the understanding of men: he scares them with such fearful visions: 
\v 17 to turn a man from unrighteousness, and he delivers his body from a fall. 
\v 18 He also spares his soul from death, and to not fall in war. 
\p
\v 19 Again, he chastens him with sickness on his bed, and the multitude of his bones is benumbed. 
\v 20 He will not be able to take any food, though his soul will desire food; 
\v 21 until his flesh is consumed, and he shows his bones bare. 
\v 22 His soul also draws near to death, and his life is in Hades. 
\v 23 Though there should be a thousand messengers of death, not one of them will wound him: if he should purpose in his heart to turn to the Lord, and declare to man his fault, and show his folly; 
\v 24 he will support him, that he should not perish, and will restore his body as \add fresh\add* plaster upon a wall; and he will fill his bones with marrow. 
\v 25 He will make his flesh tender as that of a babe, and he will restore him among men in \add his\add* full strength. 
\v 26 He will pray to the Lord, and his prayer will be accepted by him; he will enter with a cheerful face, with a full expression \add of praise\add*: for he will give to men \add their\add* due. 
\v 27 Even then a man will blame himself, saying, What kind of things have I done? And he has not punished me according to the full amount of my sins. 
\v 28 Deliver my soul, that it may not go to destruction, and my life will see the light. 
\p
\v 29 Behold, all these things the Mighty One works in a threefold manner with a man. 
\v 30 He has delivered my soul from death, that my life may praise him in the light. 
\p
\v 31 Listen, Job, and hear me: be silent, and I will speak. 
\v 32 If you have words, answer me: speak, for I desire you to be justified. 
\v 33 If not, hear me: be silent, and I will teach you. 
\c 34 
\p
\v 1 Elius continued, and said, 
\p
\v 2 Hear me, you wise men; listen, you who have knowledge. 
\v 3 For the ear tries words, and the mouth tastes food. 
\v 4 Let us choose judgment for ourselves: let us know among ourselves what is right. 
\v 5 For Job has said, I am righteous: the Lord has removed my justice. 
\v 6 He has lied about my case: my arrow is grievous without unrighteousness of mine. 
\p
\v 7 What man is as Job, drinking mockery like water? 
\v 8 \add saying\add*, I have not sinned, nor committed ungodliness, nor had fellowship with workers of wickedness, to go with the ungodly. 
\v 9 For you should not say, There will be no oversight for a man, for oversight of him is from the Lord. 
\p
\v 10 Therefore hear me, you who are wise in heart: far be it from me to sin before the Lord, and to pervert righteousness before the Almighty. 
\v 11 Yes, he renders to a man accordingly as each one does, and in a man's path he will find him. 
\p
\v 12 Do you think the Lord will do wrong, or that the Almighty will pervert judgment—he who made the earth? 
\v 13 And who is the one who made what is under heaven and all things in it?
\v 14 For if he would confine, and restrain his spirit with himself; 
\v 15 all flesh would die together, and every mortal would return to the earth, where also he was formed. 
\p
\v 16 But if not, hear these things, listen to the voice of words. 
\v 17 Behold then the one who hates iniquities, and who destroys the wicked, who is forever just. 
\p
\v 18 \add He is\add* ungodly that says to a king, You are a transgressor, \add that says\add* to princes, O most ungodly one. 
\v 19 \add Such a one\add* as would not reverence the face of a honourable man, neither knows how to give honour to the great, so as that their persons should be respected. 
\v 20 But their crying out and imploring a man will turn out empty for them, for they acted unlawfully while the helpless were being turned aside.
\v 21 For he surveys the works of men, and nothing of what they do has escaped him. 
\v 22 Neither will there be a place for the workers of wickedness to hide themselves. 
\v 23 For he will not lay anything more upon a man, for the Lord watches all
\v 24 who comprehends unsearchable things, glorious also and wondrous things without number. 
\v 25 Who discovers their works, and will bring night about \add upon them\add*, and they will be brought low. 
\v 26 He quite destroys the ungodly, for they are seen before him. 
\v 27 Because they turned aside from the law of God, and did not regard his ordinances, 
\v 28 so as to bring before him the cry of the needy; for he will hear the cry of the poor. 
\p
\v 29 He will give quiet, and who will condemn? And he will hide his face, and who will see him? Whether \add it be done\add* against a nation, or against a man also: 
\v 30 causing a hypocrite to be king, because of the waywardness of the people. 
\p
\v 31 For \add there is\add* one that says to the Mighty One, I have received \add blessings\add*; I will not take a pledge: 
\v 32 I will see apart from myself: do you show me if I have done unrighteousness; I will not do \add so\add* any more. 
\v 33 Will he take vengeance for it on you, because you will put \add it\add* far \add from you\add*? For you will choose, and not I; and what you know, speak. 
\v 34 Because the wise in heart will say this, and a wise man listens to my word. 
\v 35 But Job has not spoken with understanding, his words are not \add uttered\add* with knowledge. 
\v 36 But now learn, Job: no longer answer like the foolish: 
\v 37 lest we add to our sins: for lawlessness will be reckoned against us if we speak many words against the Lord. 
\c 35 
\p
\v 1 Elius resumed and said, 
\p
\v 2 What is this that you have considered just? Who are you, that you said, I am righteous before the Lord?
\v 3 Or will you say, “What shall I do if I sin?”
\v 4 I will give you and your three friends an answer. 
\p
\v 5 Look up to the sky and see; observe the clouds, how high \add they are\add* above you. 
\v 6 If you have sinned, what will you do? And if you have transgressed greatly, what can you do? 
\v 7 Suppose you are righteous, what will you give him? Or what will he receive from your hand? 
\v 8 Your ungodliness is for a man like yourself, and your righteousness for a son of man. 
\v 9 Being oppressed by a multitude, they cry out; they will call for help because of the arm of many. 
\v 10 But no one said, Where is God who made me, who appoints the night-watches; 
\v 11 who distinguishes me from the four-footed beasts of the earth, and from the birds of the sky? 
\v 12 There they will cry, and none will listen, because of the insolence of wicked men. 
\p
\v 13 For the Lord doesn't wish to see unlawful things, for the Almighty himself sees all things,
\v 14 those who perform lawless deeds; and he will save me: present your case before him, if you are able to praise him as he is. 
\v 15 And now, because he isn't visiting with his wrath, and he has not greatly regarded transgression,
\v 16 yet Job vainly opens his mouth, in ignorance he multiplies words. 
\c 36 
\p
\v 1 Elius further continued, and said, 
\p
\v 2 Wait for me yet a little while, that I may teach you: for there is yet speech in me. 
\p
\v 3 Having fetched my knowledge from afar, I will speak just things for my works, 
\v 4 words of truth and not unjust; you understand wrongly.
\p
\v 5 But know that the Lord will not cast off an innocent man: being mighty in strength of wisdom, 
\v 6 he will not by any means save alive the ungodly: he will give the poor justice. 
\v 7 He will not turn away his eyes from the righteous, but \add they will be\add* with kings on the throne: and he will establish them in triumph, and they will be exalted. 
\v 8 But those who are bound in shackles will be held fast in ropes of poverty. 
\v 9 He will recount to them their works, and their transgressions, for such will act with violence. 
\v 10 But he will listen to the righteous: and he has said that they will turn from unrighteousness. 
\v 11 If they should hear and serve \add him\add*, they will spend their days in prosperity, and their years in honour. 
\v 12 But he doesn't preserve the ungodly; because they are not willing to know the Lord, and because when reproved they were disobedient. 
\p
\v 13 The hypocrites in heart will array wrath \add against themselves\add*; they will not cry, because he has bound them. 
\v 14 Therefore let their soul die in youth, and their life be wounded by angels.
\v 15 Because they afflicted the weak and helpless: and he will vindicate the judgment of the meek. 
\v 16 He has also drawn you out of the mouth of the enemy: 
\v 17 a deep gulf, a rushing stream beneath it, and your table came down full of abundance. Justice will not fail from the righteous; 
\v 18 but wrath will be on the ungodly, because of the wickedness of the bribes which they received for acts of injustice. 
\p
\v 19 Don't let your mind willingly turn you aside from the petition of the helpless who are in distress because of all those exercising power. 
\v 20 Don't draw out the night, for peoples rise in their place.
\v 21 But take heed lest you do that which is wrong: because for this I have chosen from poverty. 
\p
\v 22 Behold, the Mighty One will prevail by his strength: for who is powerful as he is? 
\v 23 Who is he who examines his works? Or who can say, He has worked injustice? 
\v 24 Remember that his works are great \add beyond\add* those which men have attempted. 
\v 25 Every man has seen in himself, how many mortals are wounded. 
\v 26 Behold, the Mighty One is great, and we will not know \add him\add*: the number of his years is even infinite. 
\v 27 The drops of rain are numbered by him, and will be poured out in rain to form a cloud. 
\v 28 The ancient \add heavens\add* will flow, and the clouds overshadow innumerable mortals: 
\v 28a he has fixed a time to cattle, and they know the order of rest. 
\v 28b \add Yet\add* by all these things your understanding isn't astonished, neither is your mind disturbed in \add your\add* body. 
\v 29 Though one should understand the outspreadings of the clouds, \add or\add* the measure of his tabernacle; 
\v 30 behold he will stretch his bow against him, and he covers the bottom of the sea. 
\v 31 For by them he will judge the nations: he will give food to him that has strength. 
\v 32 He has hidden the light in \add his\add* hands, and given charge concerning it to the interposing \add cloud\add*. 
\v 33 The Lord will declare concerning this \add to\add* his friend: \add but there is\add* a portion also for unrighteousness. 
\c 37 
\p
\v 1 At this also my heart is troubled, and moved out of its place. 
\v 2 Hear a report in the anger of the Lord's wrath, and discourse will come out of his mouth. 
\v 3 His dominion is under the whole heaven, and his light is at the extremities of the earth. 
\v 4 A voice will cry out after him; he will thunder with the voice of his excellency, and he will not hold them back when one hears his voice. 
\v 5 The Mighty One will thunder wonderfully with his voice: for he has done great things which we didn't know; 
\v 6 commanding the snow, Be upon the earth, and the stormy rain, and the storm of the showers of his might. 
\v 7 He seals up the hand of every man, that every man may know his own weakness. 
\v 8 The wild beasts enter their shelter and rest in \add their\add* lair. 
\v 9 Whirlwinds come from the storehouses, and cold from the heights. 
\v 10 From the breath of the Mighty One he will send frost; and he guides the water in whatever way he pleases. 
\v 11 A cloud covers the chosen thing; his light disperses the cloud. 
\v 12 He turns the encircling masses by his \f + \fr 37:12 \fqa Gr. \ft θεεβουλαθω is obscure and may be corrupted text; 'governance' is Brenton's interpretation of this.\f*governance for their works: all that he commands them.
\v 13 these have been appointed by him on the earth, whether for correction, or for his land, or whether he finds \f + \fr 37:13 \ft or, him.\f*it for mercy. 
\p
\v 14 Listen to this, O Job: stand still, and be admonished of the power of the Lord. 
\v 15 We know that God has disposed his works, having made light out of darkness. 
\v 16 He knows the divisions of the clouds, and the remarkable overthrows of the ungodly. 
\v 17 But your robe is warm, and there is quiet upon the land. 
\v 18 Will you establish with him the ancient \add things\add*, strong as a the appearance of cast metal?
\v 19 Why teach me, what will we say to him? And let us cease from saying much. 
\v 20 Have I a book or a scribe by me, that I may stand and put man to silence? 
\p
\v 21 But the light isn't visible to all: it shines afar off in the heavens, as that which is from him in the clouds. 
\v 22 From the north \add come\add* the clouds shining like gold: in these great are the glory and honour of the Almighty; 
\v 23 and we do not find another his equal in strength: \add as for\add* him that judges justly, do you not think that he listens? 
\v 24 Therefore men will fear him; and the wise also in heart will fear him. 
\c 38 
\p
\v 1 After Elius had ceased from speaking, the Lord spoke to Job through the whirlwind and clouds, \add saying\add*, 
\p
\v 2 Who is this that hides counsel from me, and confines words in \add his\add* heart, and thinks to conceal \add them\add* from me? 
\v 3 Gird your loins like a man; I will ask you, and you answer me. 
\p
\v 4 Where were you when I founded the earth? Tell me now, if you have knowledge, 
\v 5 who set the measures of it, if you know? Or who stretched a line upon it? 
\v 6 On what are its rings fastened? And who is he who laid the corner-stone on it? 
\v 7 When the stars were made, all my angels praised me with a loud voice. 
\v 8 I shut up the sea with gates, when it rushed out, coming forth out of its mother's womb. 
\v 9 I made a cloud its clothing, and swathed it in mist. 
\v 10 I set bounds to it, surrounding it with bars and gates. 
\v 11 I said to it, Here will you come, but you will not go beyond, but your waves will be confined within you. 
\p
\v 12 Or did I order the morning light in your time; and has the morning star seen its appointed place; 
\v 13 to take hold of the wings of the earth, to shake out the ungodly from it? 
\v 14 Or did you take clay of the ground, and form a living creature, and set it with the power of speech upon the earth? 
\v 15 Have you removed light from the ungodly, and crushed the arm of the proud? 
\p
\v 16 Or have you gone to the source of the sea, and walked in the tracks of the deep? 
\v 17 Do the gates of death open to you in fear; and did the gatekeepers of Hades tremble when they saw you? 
\v 18 Have you been instructed in the breadth of what is under heaven? Tell me now, how great is it?
\p
\v 19 In what kind of a land does the light dwell? And of what kind is the place of darkness? 
\v 20 If you would bring me to their borders, and if you know their paths; 
\v 21 So then, I know that you were born at that time, and the number of your years is great. 
\p
\v 22 But have you gone to the treasuries of snow? And have you seen the treasuries of hail? 
\v 23 It is laid up for you for a time of enemies, for a day of war and battle? 
\v 24 Where does the frost come from? Or \add where\add* is the south wind scattered over the what is under heaven? 
\v 25 Who prepared a course for the violent rain, and a way for the thunders; 
\v 26 to rain upon the land where \add there is\add* no man, the wilderness, where no human dwells; so as to nourish the untrodden and uninhabited waste,
\v 27 and cause it to send forth a growth of grass?
\p
\v 28 Who is the rain's father? And who has generated the drops of dew? 
\v 29 Out of whose womb comes the ice? And who has produced the frost in the sky, 
\v 30 which descends like flowing water? Who has terrified the face of the ungodly? 
\p
\v 31 Do you understand the band of Pleias, and have you opened the barrier of Orion? 
\v 32 Or will you reveal Mazuroth in his season, and the evening star with his rays? Will you guide them? 
\v 33 Do you know the changes of heaven, or the events which take place together under heaven? 
\v 34 Will you call a cloud with your voice, and will it obey you with a violent shower of much rain? 
\v 35 Will you send lightnings, and they will go? And will they say to you, What shall we do?
\v 36 Who has given to women skill in weaving, or knowledge of embroidery? 
\v 37 Who is he who numbers the clouds in wisdom, and has bowed the heaven \add down\add* to the earth? 
\v 38 For it is spread out as dusty earth, and I have cemented it as one hewn stone to another. 
\p
\v 39 Will you hunt a prey for the lions? And satisfy the desires of the serpents? 
\v 40 For they lurk in their lairs, and lie in wait in the woods. 
\v 41 Who has prepared food for the raven? For its young ones wander and cry to the Lord, in search of food. 
\c 39 
\p
\v 1 Do you know the time when the wild rock-goats give birth, and have you watched over the labour-pains of the hinds? 
\v 2 Have you numbered the full months of their being with young, and have you relieved their pains: 
\v 3 and have you reared their young without fear; and will you bring an end to their labour-pains? 
\v 4 Their young will break away; they will multiply in offspring: they will go out and not return to them. 
\p
\v 5 Who set the wild ass free? And who released his bonds? 
\v 6 Because I made his habitation the wilderness, and the salt land his dwelling. 
\v 7 He laughs to scorn the multitude of the city, and doesn't hear the chiding of the tax-gatherer. 
\v 8 He will survey the mountains \add as\add* his pasture, and he seeks after every green thing. 
\p
\v 9 Will the \f + \fr 39:9 \fqa Gr. \ft “one-horned” (μονόκερως), traditionally “unicorn”; probably referring to a powerful wild ox or similar animal.\f*wild ox be willing to serve you, or to lie down at your manger? 
\v 10 Will you bind his yoke with thongs, or will he plough furrows for you in the plain? 
\v 11 Do you trust him, because his strength is great? And will you commit your works to him? 
\v 12 Will you believe that he will return to you your seed, and bring \add it\add* in \add to\add* your threshing-floor? 
\p
\v 13 \f + \fr 39:13 \ft This is one of the most difficult passages in the LXX Job text. In particular, ασιδα is obscure, uncertain and possibly corrupted. It is rendered here as `asida', a transliteration of the Greek which may in turn be a transliterated form of the Hebrew word חסידה “stork.”\f*The wing of the joyful bird flutters, if it should conceive the asida and the nestling,
\v 14 for she will leave her eggs in the ground, and warm them on the dust, 
\v 15 and has forgotten that the foot will scatter them, and the wild beasts of the field trample them. 
\v 16 She has treated her young harshly, as though they were not her own: she labours in vain without fear. 
\v 17 For God has withheld wisdom from her, and not given her a share in understanding. 
\v 18 In her season she will lift herself on high; she will scorn the horse and his rider. 
\p
\v 19 Have you invested the horse with strength, and clothed his neck with terror? 
\v 20 Have you clad him in perfect armour, and made his breast glorious with courage? 
\v 21 He paws exulting in the plain, and goes forth in strength into the plain. 
\v 22 He laughs to scorn a king as he meets him, and will by no means turn back from the sword. 
\v 23 The bow and sword rage proudly against him; and \add his\add* fury will swallow up the ground: 
\v 24 and he will not believe until the trumpet sounds. 
\v 25 When the trumpet sounds, he says, Aha! And afar off he smells the war with leaping and shouting. 
\p
\v 26 Does the hawk remain steady by your wisdom, having spread out her wings unmoved, looking toward the region of the south? 
\v 27 Does the eagle rise at your command, and the vulture remain sitting over his nest, 
\v 28 on a crag of a rock, and in a secret \add place\add*? 
\v 29 From there he seeks food, his eyes observe from afar. 
\v 30 His young ones are smeared in blood, and wherever the dead bodies are, immediately they are found. 
\c 40 
\p
\v 1 The Lord God answered Job, and said, 
\v 2 Will \add anyone\add* pervert justice with the Mighty One? Will he who reproves God answer it? 
\v 3 Job answered and said to the Lord, 
\v 4 Why do I yet plead? Being rebuked even while reproving the Lord: hearing such things, because I am nothing: and what will I answer to these? I will lay my hand on my mouth. 
\v 5 I have spoken once; but I will not do so a second time. 
\p
\v 6 The Lord yet again answered and spoke to Job out of the cloud,
\p
\v 7 No, gird up your loins now like a man; and I will ask you, and you answer me. 
\v 8 Do not set aside my judgment: and do you think that I have dealt with you for any other reason than to reveal you as righteous? 
\v 9 Have you an arm like the Lord's? Or do you thunder with a voice like his? 
\v 10 Assume now a lofty bearing and power; and clothe yourself with glory and honour. 
\v 11 Send forth messengers with wrath; and lay low every haughty one. 
\v 12 Bring down also the proud man; and consume at once the ungodly. 
\v 13 Hide them together in the earth; and fill their faces with shame. 
\v 14 \add Then\add* I will confess that your right hand can save.
\p
\v 15 But now look at the \f + \fr 40:15 \fqa Gr. \ft θηρία “great beasts” or “wild beasts”; here referring to a particular mighty creature.\f*Theria with you; they eat grass like oxen. 
\v 16 Behold now, his strength is in his loins, and his force is in the navel of his belly. 
\v 17 He sets up his tail like a cypress; and his sinews are knit together. 
\v 18 His sides are sides of brass; and his backbone is cast iron. 
\v 19 This is the chief of the creation of the Lord; made to be played with by his angels. 
\v 20 When he has gone up to a steep mountain, he causes joy to the animals in the deep. 
\v 21 He lies under trees of every kind, by the papyrus, and reed, and bulrush. 
\v 22 The great trees make a shadow over him with their branches, and \add so do\add* the bushes of the field. 
\v 23 If there should be a flood, he will not perceive it; he trusts that Jordan will rush up into his mouth. 
\v 24 By his eye he will receive him; with a hook he will pierce his nose.
\p
\v 25 But will you catch the \f + \fr 40:25 \fqa Gr. \ft δράκων, a term for a great or formidable serpent-like creature, traditionally translated as 'dragon' but not intended to signify a mythical beast.\f*serpent with a hook, and put a halter about his nose? 
\v 26 Or will you fasten a ring in his nostril, and bore his lip with a clasp? 
\v 27 Will he address you with a petition? Softly, with the voice of a suppliant? 
\v 28 Will he make a covenant with you? And will you take him for a perpetual servant? 
\v 29 Will you play with him as with a bird? Or bind him as a sparrow for a child? 
\v 30 Do the nations feed upon him, and the nations of the Phœnicians share him? 
\v 31 All the ships come together would not be able to bear the mere skin of his tail, nor his head in fishing-vessels. 
\v 32 Lay your hand on him; remember the battle that takes place in his body, and do it no more.
\c 41 
\p
\v 1 Have you not seen him and wondered at what has been said?
\v 2 Do you not fear because I am prepared? For who has resisted me? 
\v 3 Or who will resist me and endure, since everything under heaven is mine? 
\p
\v 4 I will not be silent concerning him nor about the matter of his power;  \add anyone\add* would pity his equal. 
\v 5 Who will open the face of his garment? And who can enter within the fold of his breast-plate? 
\v 6 Who will open the gates of his face? Terror surrounds his teeth. 
\v 7 His inwards are bronze plates, and his joints are like emery stone. 
\v 8 One \add part\add* clings fast to another, and the air cannot come between them. 
\v 9 They will remain united each to the other: they are closely joined, and cannot be separated. 
\v 10 At his sneezing light flashes forth, and his eyes are like the morning star. 
\v 11 Out of his mouth proceed as it were burning lamps, and as it were hearths of fire are cast abroad. 
\v 12 Out of his nostrils proceeds smoke of a furnace burning with fire of coals. 
\v 13 His breath is live coals, and a flame goes out of his mouth. 
\v 14 Power is lodged in his neck, before him destruction runs. 
\v 15 The flesh also of his body is joined together: pour out upon him; he will not be shaken. 
\v 16 His heart is firm as a stone, and it stands like an unyielding anvil. 
\v 17 When he turns, fear to the four-footed beasts that leap on the earth. 
\v 18 If spears come against him, a raised spear and breastplate will accomplish nothing.
\v 19 For he considers iron as chaff, and brass as rotten wood. 
\v 20 The bronze bow will surely not wound him, he counts a slinger as grass. 
\v 21 Hammers are reckoned as stubble; and he laughs at the waving of the firebrand. 
\v 22 His bed is sharp spikes; and all the gold of the sea under him is like immeasurable clay. 
\v 23 He makes the deep boil like a bronze cauldron; and he regards the sea as a bowl of ointment, 
\v 24 and the deepest part of the abyss as a captive: he treats the abyss as a walkway.
\v 25 There is nothing on earth like him, formed to be toyed with by my angels. 
\v 26 He beholds every high thing: and he is king of all that are in the waters. 
\c 42 
\p
\v 1 Then Job answered and said to the Lord, 
\p
\v 2 I know that you can do all things, and nothing is impossible with you. 
\v 3 For who is he who hides counsel from you? Or who keeps back his words, and thinks to hide them from you? And who will tell me what I didn't know, great and wonderful things which I didn't understand? 
\p
\v 4 But hear me, O Lord, that I also may speak: and I will ask you, and do you teach me. 
\v 5 I have heard the report of you by the ear before; but now my eye has seen you. 
\v 6 Therefore I have counted myself as nothing, and have melted away: and I regard myself as dust and ashes. 
\p
\v 7 It happened after the Lord had spoken all these words to Job, \add that\add* the Lord said to Eliphaz the Thæmanite, You have sinned, and your two friends: for you have not said anything true before me, as my servant Job \add has\add*. 
\v 8 Now then take seven bullocks, and seven rams, and go to my servant Job, and he will offer a burnt-offering for you. And my servant Job will pray for you, for I will only accept him: for but for his sake, I would have destroyed you, for you have not spoken the truth against my servant Job. 
\p
\v 9 So Eliphaz the Thæmanite, and Baldad the Sauchite, and Sophar the Minæan, went and did as the Lord commanded them: and he pardoned their sin for the sake of Job. 
\p
\v 10 The Lord prospered Job: and when he prayed also for his friends, he forgave them \add their\add* sin: and the Lord gave Job twice as much, even the double of what he had before. 
\v 11 All his brothers and sisters heard all that had happened to him, and they came to him, along with all who had known him before: and they ate and drank with him, and comforted him, and wondered at all that the Lord had brought upon him: and each one gave him a ewe-lamb, and four drachmas of unminted gold.
\p
\v 12 The Lord blessed Job's latter days, more than the beginning: and his livestock were fourteen thousand sheep, six thousand camels, a thousand yoke of oxen, a thousand grazing she-donkeys. 
\v 13 Seven sons and three daughters were born to him. 
\v 14 He called the first, Day, and the second, \f + \fr 42:14 \ft Cassia — an aromatic spice/tree related to cinnamon.\f*Cassia, and the third, \f + \fr 42:14 \ft Horn of Amaltheia — an allusion to the horn of abundance (cornucopia) in Greek tradition.\f*Horn of Amaltheia. 
\v 15 No women under heaven were found fairer than the daughters of Job: and their father gave them an inheritance along with their brothers. 
\p
\v 16 Job lived after \add his\add* affliction a hundred and seventy years: and all the years he lived were two hundred and forty: and Job saw his sons and his sons' sons, the fourth generation. 
\v 17 Job died, an old man and full of days: 
\v 17a and it is written that he will rise again with those whom the Lord raises up. 
\p
\v 17b This man is described in the Syriac book \add as\add* living in the land of Ausis, on the borders of Idumea and Arabia: and his name before was Jobab; 
\v 17c and having taken an Arabian wife, he begot a son whose name was Ennon. And he himself was the son of his father Zare, one of the sons of Esau, and of his mother Bosorrha, so that he was the fifth from Abraham. 
\v 17d These were the kings who reigned in Edom, which country he also ruled over: first, Balac, the son of Beor, and the name of his city was Dennaba: but after Balac, Jobab, who is called Job: and after him Asom, who was governor out of the country of Thæman: and after him Adad, the son of Barad, who destroyed Midian in the plain of Moab; and the name of his city was Gethaim. 
\v 17e And \add his\add* friends who came to him were Eliphaz, of the children of Esau, king of the Thæmanites, Baldad sovereign of the Sauchæans, Sophar king of the Minæans. 
